WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.980 --> 00:00:03.922
Taylor Swift just dropped
a star-studded music video

00:00:03.922 --> 00:00:06.717
for the second single
off her upcoming album.

00:00:06.717 --> 00:00:08.720
"You Need to Calm Down" shows a playful

00:00:08.720 --> 00:00:10.895
yet political side of the pop star,

00:00:10.895 --> 00:00:14.336
and in classic T-Swift fashion,
both the song and video

00:00:14.336 --> 00:00:17.640
are filled with hidden details,
references, and meanings.

00:00:17.640 --> 00:00:19.480
Here's everything you missed in the lyrics

00:00:19.480 --> 00:00:22.040
and video for "You Need to Calm Down."

00:00:22.040 --> 00:00:24.140
The video begins with an overhead shot

00:00:24.140 --> 00:00:27.030
of Taylor wearing an
eye mask and lingerie.

00:00:27.030 --> 00:00:29.750
The outfit references one
of the most memorable looks

00:00:29.750 --> 00:00:32.040
from her "Blank Space" music video.

00:00:32.040 --> 00:00:35.720
We see table spreads with
several seemingly random objects.

00:00:35.720 --> 00:00:37.460
But this is Taylor we're talking about,

00:00:37.460 --> 00:00:40.210
so let's take a closer look.

00:00:40.210 --> 00:00:42.750
The dice on the left here add up to 13,

00:00:42.750 --> 00:00:44.540
Taylor's known lucky number.

00:00:44.540 --> 00:00:47.340
The next table has six
pieces of candy on it,

00:00:47.340 --> 00:00:49.770
which could be referencing
the month of June.

00:00:49.770 --> 00:00:51.390
June, of course, is Pride Month

00:00:51.390 --> 00:00:52.910
and something Taylor's celebrating

00:00:52.910 --> 00:00:54.920
in her new song and video.

00:00:54.920 --> 00:00:56.850
And when you combine the six with the 13

00:00:56.850 --> 00:01:00.400
from the dice, you get 6/13, or June 13,

00:01:00.400 --> 00:01:04.320
the day Taylor announced her
upcoming video and album dates.

00:01:04.320 --> 00:01:06.020
On the wall, there's a framed quote

00:01:06.020 --> 00:01:08.820
from a Cher interview from 1996.

00:01:08.820 --> 00:01:09.850
Cher: "You know, sweetheart, one day you

00:01:09.850 --> 00:01:13.070
should settle down and marry a rich man."

00:01:13.070 --> 00:01:15.010
And I said, "Mom, I am a rich man."

00:01:15.010 --> 00:01:16.030
[Narrator] The quote is in line

00:01:16.030 --> 00:01:18.560
with feminist themes
Taylor's song explores,

00:01:18.560 --> 00:01:20.050
which we'll get to.

00:01:20.050 --> 00:01:22.414
Taylor looks down at a watch as she sings,

00:01:22.414 --> 00:01:24.400
Swift: It's 7 a.m.

00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:26.380
[Narrator] We see the time set to 7 a.m.,

00:01:26.380 --> 00:01:28.200
another nod to her seventh album,

00:01:28.200 --> 00:01:31.740
as well as the face of her
newest kitten, Benjamin Button.

00:01:31.740 --> 00:01:33.900
On the watch, the number 1 is replaced

00:01:33.900 --> 00:01:36.180
with Taylor's lucky number 13.

00:01:36.180 --> 00:01:38.040
When Taylor throws her phone to the bed,

00:01:38.040 --> 00:01:40.260
we see her case has the
word "Lover" written out

00:01:40.260 --> 00:01:42.890
in the same font as the album's artwork.

00:01:42.890 --> 00:01:45.780
The sparkly case also matches
the outfit Taylor wore

00:01:45.780 --> 00:01:48.810
to the iHeartRadio Awards,
her first red carpet

00:01:48.810 --> 00:01:50.200
of the "Lover" era.

00:01:50.200 --> 00:01:51.870
The phone sparks into flames,

00:01:51.870 --> 00:01:54.390
hinting that Taylor's
finally over all the drama

00:01:54.390 --> 00:01:57.670
that her phone's caused her,
aka her recorded phone call

00:01:57.670 --> 00:01:59.420
with Kim and Kanye.

00:01:59.420 --> 00:02:01.040
On the wall to the right of Taylor

00:02:01.040 --> 00:02:02.650
there's a butterfly picture.

00:02:02.650 --> 00:02:05.130
Butterflies are another
image Taylor's been using

00:02:05.130 --> 00:02:06.716
to promote her new era.

00:02:07.930 --> 00:02:10.840
Next, we see Taylor
outside of the trailer.

00:02:10.840 --> 00:02:12.775
We catch a glimpse of the back tattoo

00:02:12.775 --> 00:02:14.960
from the song's artwork,
a snake surrounded

00:02:14.960 --> 00:02:16.850
by lots of butterflies.

00:02:16.850 --> 00:02:19.300
The tattoo also matches the opening image

00:02:19.300 --> 00:02:21.150
of the video for "Me."

00:02:21.150 --> 00:02:23.400
Taylor's wearing heart-shaped earrings.

00:02:23.400 --> 00:02:26.010
If they look familiar,
it's because Katy Perry

00:02:26.010 --> 00:02:29.370
wore a nearly identical
pair in October 2018,

00:02:29.370 --> 00:02:30.960
but more on Katy later.

00:02:30.960 --> 00:02:33.900
And, of course, there are
exactly 13 rhinestones

00:02:33.900 --> 00:02:36.070
on top of Taylor's sunglasses.

00:02:36.070 --> 00:02:37.890
When Taylor walks away, we can see

00:02:37.890 --> 00:02:39.820
that her trailer is on fire.

00:02:39.820 --> 00:02:41.830
This could symbolize Taylor setting fire

00:02:41.830 --> 00:02:43.910
to her "Blank Space"-era persona,

00:02:43.910 --> 00:02:46.820
when she was at the height
of her media visibility.

00:02:46.820 --> 00:02:49.733
As the inside of her
trailer burns, Taylor sings,

00:02:49.733 --> 00:02:52.974
Swift: And snakes and stones
never broke my bones so.

00:02:52.974 --> 00:02:54.100
[Narrator] a reference to the people

00:02:54.100 --> 00:02:57.410
who tried to bring Taylor
down by calling her a snake.

00:02:57.410 --> 00:02:59.510
We then see Taylor floating in a pool,

00:02:59.510 --> 00:03:01.270
which is covered in roses.

00:03:01.270 --> 00:03:04.112
At her 2019 Wango Tango performance,

00:03:04.112 --> 00:03:05.900
Taylor said that she brought an Easter egg

00:03:05.900 --> 00:03:07.182
on stage with her.

00:03:07.182 --> 00:03:10.950
The stage was decorated
with, you guessed it, roses.

00:03:10.950 --> 00:03:14.220
The video goes on to reveal
the rest of the trailer park.

00:03:14.220 --> 00:03:16.110
If you look closely, you'll see a sign

00:03:16.110 --> 00:03:18.800
that says 16th Avenue,
which could be a nod

00:03:18.800 --> 00:03:22.610
to Nashville's 16th Avenue,
also known as Music Row.

00:03:22.610 --> 00:03:26.060
We see the first of the
song's many LGBTQ nods,

00:03:26.060 --> 00:03:28.680
with the entrance
decorated with Pride flags.

00:03:28.680 --> 00:03:31.670
The song's overall pro-LGBTQ message

00:03:31.670 --> 00:03:33.290
could be aimed at President Trump

00:03:33.290 --> 00:03:36.770
and his administration's
anti-LGBTQ policies,

00:03:36.770 --> 00:03:38.760
as the song "You Need to Calm Down"

00:03:38.760 --> 00:03:41.640
was released on Trump's birthday.

00:03:41.640 --> 00:03:44.670
We get our first celebrity
cameo with Dexter Mayfield,

00:03:44.670 --> 00:03:46.500
a queer, plus-size model.

00:03:46.500 --> 00:03:48.900
YouTube comedian Hannah
Hart can also be seen

00:03:48.900 --> 00:03:51.390
in the background holding up a boom box.

00:03:51.390 --> 00:03:53.570
There's a mailbox labeled
"love letters only"

00:03:53.570 --> 00:03:55.360
in Taylor's handwriting.

00:03:55.360 --> 00:03:57.900
At the next trailer, we finally find out

00:03:57.900 --> 00:04:01.140
what Taylor's mysterious
orange-fence post was about.

00:04:01.140 --> 00:04:04.130
All the fences in the
video could be another dig

00:04:04.130 --> 00:04:06.810
at Trump, whose platform for immigration

00:04:06.810 --> 00:04:09.280
involves building fences and walls.

00:04:09.280 --> 00:04:12.110
There are more Pride flags
hanging behind the orange trailer

00:04:12.110 --> 00:04:14.070
and the word "rent" in a window,

00:04:14.070 --> 00:04:16.330
which could be referencing
the iconic musical

00:04:16.330 --> 00:04:19.800
of the same name, best known
for dealing with queer issues.

00:04:19.800 --> 00:04:21.870
The next cameo is Laverne Cox,

00:04:21.870 --> 00:04:24.420
a transgender actress and activist.

00:04:24.420 --> 00:04:26.680
Chester Lockhart, a gay actor and singer,

00:04:26.680 --> 00:04:28.800
swoons at the sight of her.

00:04:28.800 --> 00:04:30.870
We then see Taylor in a blue-toned wig

00:04:30.870 --> 00:04:34.370
that ombrés to match the
colors of the bisexual flag.

00:04:34.370 --> 00:04:36.720
Taylor's jacket is decorated with stars,

00:04:36.720 --> 00:04:39.680
another popular motif in her album promo.

00:04:39.680 --> 00:04:43.440
She's also wearing her lucky
number 13 on her necklace.

00:04:43.440 --> 00:04:45.750
Taylor walks up with her longtime BFF

00:04:45.750 --> 00:04:48.020
and the video's coproducer Todrick Hall,

00:04:48.020 --> 00:04:50.800
who's a gay singer, dancer, and actor.

00:04:50.800 --> 00:04:53.570
Todrick's robe is covered
in glittery hearts,

00:04:53.570 --> 00:04:55.710
similar to the ones from
Taylor's album cover

00:04:55.710 --> 00:04:57.600
and in the "Mean" music video.

00:04:57.600 --> 00:05:00.790
Gay pop star Hayley
Kiyoko is the next cameo.

00:05:00.790 --> 00:05:02.330
She's shooting an arrow aimed

00:05:02.330 --> 00:05:04.880
at a target labeled with the number 5.

00:05:04.880 --> 00:05:08.450
Five could be a reference to
Taylor's fan-loved track fives.

00:05:08.450 --> 00:05:11.360
"Track 5" was also written
on a button Taylor wore

00:05:11.360 --> 00:05:13.840
on her Entertainment
Weekly cover back in May,

00:05:13.840 --> 00:05:16.340
all of which she confirmed
were Easter eggs.

00:05:16.340 --> 00:05:19.100
Could "Track 5" be Taylor's next single?

00:05:19.100 --> 00:05:21.370
The video goes on to reveal protesters

00:05:21.370 --> 00:05:23.900
holding up anti-LGBTQ signs,

00:05:23.900 --> 00:05:26.720
leaning in to the political
tones of the song.

00:05:26.720 --> 00:05:29.547
The first protest sign we
see says "Homasekuality

00:05:29.547 --> 00:05:32.600
is a sin," with
"homosexuality" spelled wrong.

00:05:32.600 --> 00:05:34.350
This might be Taylor's way of saying

00:05:34.350 --> 00:05:37.510
that homophobic people
need to educate themselves.

00:05:37.510 --> 00:05:39.600
We get to a celebration of gay love

00:05:39.600 --> 00:05:42.380
with a wedding between
actor Jesse Tyler Ferguson

00:05:42.380 --> 00:05:44.900
and his real-life husband, Justin Mikita.

00:05:44.900 --> 00:05:47.280
The wedding is officiated by Ciara.

00:05:47.280 --> 00:05:49.507
We see another protest sign that says

00:05:49.507 --> 00:05:51.960
"Adam + Eve not Adam + Steve,"

00:05:51.960 --> 00:05:55.430
which is a saying frequently
used by conservative Christians

00:05:55.430 --> 00:05:59.080
and could be Taylor calling
out religious conservatives.

00:05:59.080 --> 00:06:01.940
Our first "Queer Eye" cameo
comes from Tan France,

00:06:01.940 --> 00:06:05.470
who walks by with a teapot
and dramatically sips tea.

00:06:05.470 --> 00:06:07.690
The video pans over to Taylor sipping tea

00:06:07.690 --> 00:06:10.630
with Todrick and the rest
of the "Queer Eye" cast.

00:06:10.630 --> 00:06:13.900
"Tea" has become a popular
reference to throwing shade,

00:06:13.900 --> 00:06:17.430
and the phrase "spilling tea"
comes from black drag culture,

00:06:17.430 --> 00:06:20.970
another aspect of the LGBTQ
community Taylor celebrates

00:06:20.970 --> 00:06:22.100
in her video.

00:06:22.100 --> 00:06:24.340
Both the tea set and Jonathan's dress

00:06:24.340 --> 00:06:26.010
were teased on Taylor's Instagram

00:06:26.010 --> 00:06:27.720
before the video release.

00:06:27.720 --> 00:06:29.660
The tea set also looks identical

00:06:29.660 --> 00:06:31.390
to one Taylor used in a sketch

00:06:31.390 --> 00:06:34.230
on "Ellen" in 2012, which
could be an Easter egg

00:06:34.230 --> 00:06:36.570
for Ellen's soon-to-be scene cameo.

00:06:36.570 --> 00:06:37.480
Our next cameo

00:06:37.480 --> 00:06:41.120
is Olympic figure skater Adam
Rippon serving snow cones.

00:06:41.120 --> 00:06:45.180
Rippon is one of the few
openly gay Olympic athletes.

00:06:45.180 --> 00:06:47.140
Ellen DeGeneres makes her brief appearance

00:06:47.140 --> 00:06:49.420
while getting a tattoo from Adam Lambert.

00:06:49.420 --> 00:06:52.000
The tattoo looks like it
spells out "Cruel Summer,"

00:06:52.000 --> 00:06:55.410
which could be a reference
to the 1984 self-love anthem

00:06:55.410 --> 00:06:58.550
by Bananarama, or one
last dig at Kanye West,

00:06:58.550 --> 00:07:01.600
who released a compilation
album of the same name.

00:07:01.600 --> 00:07:04.520
Ellen secretly sported the
tattoo under her jacket

00:07:04.520 --> 00:07:07.610
during her recent interview
with Taylor on her show.

00:07:07.610 --> 00:07:09.690
The video goes on to show Taylor tanning

00:07:09.690 --> 00:07:11.390
while wearing heart-shaped sunglasses,

00:07:11.390 --> 00:07:13.400
which were featured in her lyric video,

00:07:13.400 --> 00:07:16.460
and the straw in the cup
next to her says "Lover."

00:07:16.460 --> 00:07:18.330
We see the protesters again.

00:07:18.330 --> 00:07:21.420
One of the protesters' signs
says "Get a brain, morans!"

00:07:21.420 --> 00:07:24.480
with "morons" spelled
wrong, reiterating the idea

00:07:24.480 --> 00:07:27.680
that protesters need
to educate themselves.

00:07:27.680 --> 00:07:30.940
The iconic Billy Porter walks
in front of the protesters

00:07:30.940 --> 00:07:33.560
as Taylor sings, "Don't step on his gown,"

00:07:33.560 --> 00:07:34.660
which could be a reference

00:07:34.660 --> 00:07:36.840
to when Billy faced homophobic backlash

00:07:36.840 --> 00:07:38.906
for wearing a gown to the Oscars.

00:07:43.550 --> 00:07:45.460
On the song's bridge, Taylor shifts

00:07:45.460 --> 00:07:48.910
from LGBTQ pride to
feminist pride, singing,

00:07:48.910 --> 00:07:51.902
Swift: And we see you over
there on the internet,

00:07:51.902 --> 00:07:54.220
comparing all the girls
who are killing it.

00:07:54.220 --> 00:07:55.780
[Narrator] The video's accompanying scene

00:07:55.780 --> 00:07:57.120
is a pop-queen pageant.

00:07:57.120 --> 00:08:00.210
Stars from "RuPaul's Drag
Race" play iconic pop queens

00:08:00.210 --> 00:08:02.830
that the media constantly
compares each other to.

00:08:02.830 --> 00:08:05.980
Let's take a closer look
at each star in the lineup.

00:08:05.980 --> 00:08:09.212
First, we see Tatianna as Ariana Grande.

00:08:09.212 --> 00:08:12.570
Grande and Swift are two of
music's biggest record-breakers,

00:08:12.570 --> 00:08:15.820
and the media loves comparing
the singers to one another.

00:08:15.820 --> 00:08:18.040
Trinity The Tuck is Lady Gaga.

00:08:18.040 --> 00:08:20.460
In a 2014 interview with Howard Stern,

00:08:20.460 --> 00:08:23.714
Gaga commented that she used
to think Taylor was full of...

00:08:23.714 --> 00:08:25.280
but instantly changed her mind

00:08:25.280 --> 00:08:26.570
once she met Taylor.

00:08:26.570 --> 00:08:30.210
However, the quote was spun
into a feud between the stars.

00:08:30.210 --> 00:08:33.320
Delta Work is Adele, another
musician whose awards

00:08:33.320 --> 00:08:35.840
and records the media
constantly keeps score of

00:08:35.840 --> 00:08:37.140
against Taylor's.

00:08:37.140 --> 00:08:39.500
Trinity K. Bonet is Cardi B.

00:08:39.500 --> 00:08:41.940
In 2017, Cardi's "Bodak Yellow"

00:08:41.940 --> 00:08:43.740
knocked Taylor's "Look
What You Made Me Do"

00:08:43.740 --> 00:08:45.240
from the top of the charts.

00:08:45.240 --> 00:08:47.410
Taylor was quick to knock down any rumors

00:08:47.410 --> 00:08:49.560
of bad blood and sent the rapper flowers,

00:08:49.560 --> 00:08:52.120
congratulating her for the song's success.

00:08:52.120 --> 00:08:54.030
There's Jade Jolie as Taylor,

00:08:54.030 --> 00:08:56.756
and the next is Riley Knoxx as Beyoncé.

00:08:56.756 --> 00:08:59.060
Taylor was most recently
compared to Beyoncé

00:08:59.060 --> 00:09:00.470
after her performance of "Me"

00:09:00.470 --> 00:09:03.150
at the 2019 Billboard Music Awards,

00:09:03.150 --> 00:09:04.500
which many people called out

00:09:04.500 --> 00:09:07.300
as copying Beyoncé's
"Beychella" performance.

00:09:07.300 --> 00:09:09.040
Not to mention Beyoncé was one

00:09:09.040 --> 00:09:11.100
of the first female entertainers Taylor

00:09:11.100 --> 00:09:13.610
was ever pitted against, by Kanye West,

00:09:13.610 --> 00:09:16.330
during the infamous
VMAs acceptance speech.

00:09:16.330 --> 00:09:18.390
Adore Delano is Katy Perry.

00:09:18.390 --> 00:09:19.925
The singer is the subject of one

00:09:19.925 --> 00:09:22.080
of Taylor's most well-known feuds,

00:09:22.080 --> 00:09:23.740
but the pair reconciled last year

00:09:23.740 --> 00:09:25.750
with a literal olive branch.

00:09:25.750 --> 00:09:28.210
Still, there's a constant
media cycle devoted

00:09:28.210 --> 00:09:30.810
to their feud and reconciliation.

00:09:30.810 --> 00:09:34.500
Last, we have A'keria C.
Davenport as Nicki Minaj.

00:09:34.500 --> 00:09:36.480
Taylor and Nicki butted heads on Twitter

00:09:36.480 --> 00:09:38.360
when Taylor thought Nicki was shading her

00:09:38.360 --> 00:09:40.280
in a tweet before the VMAs.

00:09:40.280 --> 00:09:42.970
But they quickly made up
with a joint performance.

00:09:42.970 --> 00:09:45.090
In her tweets, Taylor brought up the idea

00:09:45.090 --> 00:09:46.930
of pitting women against women,

00:09:46.930 --> 00:09:49.610
which relates to the
lyrics during this scene.

00:09:49.610 --> 00:09:51.680
It's also worth noting
that the lineup here

00:09:51.680 --> 00:09:54.180
draws parallels to the
lineup of old Taylors

00:09:54.180 --> 00:09:56.580
from the "Look What You
Made Me Do" music video,

00:09:56.580 --> 00:09:58.400
each representing a version of Taylor

00:09:58.400 --> 00:10:00.600
that was criticized by the media.

00:10:00.600 --> 00:10:02.890
RuPaul enters the pageant with a crown,

00:10:02.890 --> 00:10:05.990
one of the video teasers
from Taylor's Instagram.

00:10:05.990 --> 00:10:08.800
He tosses it into the air,
and no one reaches for it,

00:10:08.800 --> 00:10:10.199
representing the lyrics

00:10:10.199 --> 00:10:11.780
Swift: We all got crowns.

00:10:11.780 --> 00:10:13.510
[Narrator] This line is also a reference

00:10:13.510 --> 00:10:16.110
to Taylor's "Reputation"
track "Call It What You Want,"

00:10:16.110 --> 00:10:17.317
on which she sings,

00:10:17.317 --> 00:10:20.190
Swift: They took the
crown, but it's all right.

00:10:20.190 --> 00:10:22.690
[Narrator] in reference to
her crumbling reputation.

00:10:22.690 --> 00:10:24.840
Here, Taylor's realizing there's space

00:10:24.840 --> 00:10:26.173
for everyone at the top.

00:10:28.560 --> 00:10:30.040
Rather than fight for the crown,

00:10:30.040 --> 00:10:31.740
the pop queens all dance together

00:10:31.740 --> 00:10:33.680
with the trailer-park residents.

00:10:33.680 --> 00:10:35.670
They're dancing in a
colorful mess of cake,

00:10:35.670 --> 00:10:38.010
similar to how Taylor danced
through a rainbow mess

00:10:38.010 --> 00:10:40.100
at the end of the video for "Me."

00:10:40.100 --> 00:10:42.170
But here, Taylor isn't dancing.

00:10:42.170 --> 00:10:44.140
Instead, she's dressed as french fries

00:10:44.140 --> 00:10:46.880
and appears to be searching
for someone in the crowd.

00:10:46.880 --> 00:10:48.140
The image looks like a scene

00:10:48.140 --> 00:10:50.830
from her music video for
"I Knew You Were Trouble,"

00:10:50.830 --> 00:10:52.500
where she's searching
through a dancing crowd

00:10:52.500 --> 00:10:55.420
for a guy, sporting
similar pink-dyed hair.

00:10:55.420 --> 00:10:58.000
But Taylor doesn't really
need a man anymore.

00:10:58.000 --> 00:11:01.830
Instead, who she finds is
none other than Katy Perry.

00:11:01.830 --> 00:11:03.450
Katy's dressed as a burger,

00:11:03.450 --> 00:11:05.950
the perfect pair to Taylor's fries.

00:11:05.950 --> 00:11:08.010
Fans had hoped that Katy
would make an appearance

00:11:08.010 --> 00:11:10.570
in the video, after spotting
those heart-shaped earrings

00:11:10.570 --> 00:11:12.577
in the song's cover art.

00:11:12.577 --> 00:11:15.020
Katy's burger dress is
the same one she wore

00:11:15.020 --> 00:11:16.520
to the Met Gala in May.

00:11:16.520 --> 00:11:19.720
While Katy and Taylor hug and
the other characters dance,

00:11:19.720 --> 00:11:21.640
we see Ryan Reynolds painting a picture

00:11:21.640 --> 00:11:23.100
of the Stonewall Inn,

00:11:23.100 --> 00:11:25.600
where Taylor gave a surprise
performance the weekend

00:11:25.600 --> 00:11:27.140
before the video dropped.

00:11:27.140 --> 00:11:29.390
The NYC landmark is known as the place

00:11:29.390 --> 00:11:32.830
where the gay-rights
movement began in 1969.

00:11:32.830 --> 00:11:35.410
Ryan's inspiration board
also includes images

00:11:35.410 --> 00:11:37.090
from Pride parades.

00:11:37.090 --> 00:11:39.270
The video closes out with a call to action

00:11:39.270 --> 00:11:42.610
to support the Equality Act,
a bill currently in Congress

00:11:42.610 --> 00:11:45.260
that, if passed, would
amend the Civil Rights Act

00:11:45.260 --> 00:11:47.460
to prohibit discrimination on the basis

00:11:47.460 --> 00:11:50.090
of sexual orientation and gender identity.

00:11:50.090 --> 00:11:51.820
Taylor previously referenced the act

00:11:51.820 --> 00:11:54.150
in the lyric video for
"You Need to Calm Down"

00:11:54.150 --> 00:11:55.960
and publicly shared the letter she penned

00:11:55.960 --> 00:11:59.217
to her Tennessee state
senator about the act.

00:11:59.217 --> 00:12:01.940
"You Need to Calm Down" is
Taylor's first musical effort

00:12:01.940 --> 00:12:03.410
at politics.

00:12:03.410 --> 00:12:05.350
Although Taylor has described "Lover"

00:12:05.350 --> 00:12:08.940
as a very romantic album
in both content and sound,

00:12:08.940 --> 00:12:11.780
so far her new music seems
to be about empowerment

00:12:11.780 --> 00:12:13.400
and spreading positivity.

00:12:13.400 --> 00:12:16.110
Did we miss anything in
the lyrics or music video?

00:12:16.110 --> 00:12:18.451
Let us know in the comments below.

